## Validation plan and assumptions likely violated

Evaluate with rank metrics (Spearman correlation, NDCG@k), because rankings from social data are more reliable than absolute counts.

**Ground-truth options, cheapest first:**

- Google Popular Times as a relative busyness label, validated against Wi-Fi counts (Timokhin et al. 2020).
- Explicit Swarm "with" group check-ins to validate the group heuristic (Ayala-Gomez et al. 2017).
- Wi-Fi or Bluetooth probe sensors at a sample of bars, calibrated with manual head counts (Versichele et al. 2012; Schauer et al. 2014).
- Door counters or point-of-sale logs from partner venues.
- Ticketed events where attendance is known.

| Assumption | Why it fails here | Fix |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Closure (population fixed during sampling) | Bar crowds turn over within hours | Within-night closure, or open models (Dail & Madsen 2011) |
| Independent detection | Friends post together; badges add incentives | Beta-binomial or group-level detection (Martin et al. 2011; Clement et al. 2017) |
| Random sampling of users | Untappd users are self-selected craft-beer enthusiasts; bias varies by venue type | Post-stratification (Giorgi et al. 2022); state the estimand; report rankings |
| Constant sampling rate | Rates drift over time and space | Time- and area-varying rate (Li et al. 2024) |
| Poisson arrivals | Groups and rounding distort tests | Cluster models; correct rounding first (Kim & Whitt 2014) |

